A few post JH macro opinions that feel out of consensus ATM:

1) The core of the FOMC - Waller, Williams, Powell - are in control. Warsh is calculating how to handle this.
2) There is little signal in the 30y yield (10y is key).
3) Hiking will be a policy mistake.

@PTTechToolsLLC Why did this not happen when hike odds went up? What was the last hike cycle where the 10y yield fell?

@AlexCorrino @PTTechToolsLLC Indeed…

@ces921 Waller speech this week will be key. I’m guessing he will be hawkish and I’m guessing warsh knows this

My view is that the "cancer" spread due to fiscal and can be traced back to the covid fiscal response + subsequent pro-cyclical deficit spending. The "man on the street" is not mad b/c cpi is currently  >2%...He's mad b/c the house that costs $250k in his mind is now $600k and the cost to finance it is ~7% not 4%. The world changed over night. The anger is rooted in this change.

Wrt current Fed policy, they are restricting the areas that matter to the general public. To hike enough to slow the AI buildout would require - almost by definition - sacrificing the labor side of the dual mandate. The only thing worse than an inflation shock (which is really behind us now) is an inflation shock followed by a recession/market crash. Not to mention the fact that the rise in oil price is supply-induced and constitutes a tightening (i.e. not something the Fed should respond to).

With that said, the AI build out is meaningful and has/is shifting the "neutral" rate higher. I think that (along w/ H1 fiscal stim) is what steepened the yield curve and signaled that maybe 3.75% is roughly neutral. Tough environment for monetary authorities but holding here seems wise.

Just my rambling 2cents fwiw

There are parts of the economy that are booming (AI capex) and parts of the economy that are struggling (housing). It's a complicated issue, but I'd argue that hiking rates enough to even slow the booming area of the economy will - almost by definition - cause the Fed to sacrifice the labor side of their dual mandate. I do acknowledge that the neutral rate is rising due to the AI buildout.

I'd disagree...Hard to argue that those cuts did much to change the outlook and it's not like the labor market was/is v tight (see revisions). The real policy mistake was the Iran War...Notice that at the start of the war Dec 27 SOFR had three cuts priced still...Multiple hikes today. W/ that said, it is all debatable and I understand the perspective.

